  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a qualitative sample of seven transport companies within a single national context, which may limit generalizability to logistics sectors with different regulatory frameworks or labor market conditions.",
      "what": "This comparative case study analyzes how truck drivers and management negotiate career longevity and progression within the Dutch transport sector. The researchers conducted 31 interviews with truck drivers, direct supervisors, HR managers, and directors across seven different transport companies to examine how occupational constraints and organizational structures shape driver agency.",
      "why": "It reveals an agency paradox where drivers feel a strong sense of day-to-day job autonomy, yet this perceived control is identity-driven and fails to translate into long-term career development because employers expect self-reliance without providing the necessary structural support or resources."
    }
